  "seed_sha256": "7a67ae03b51f500cfbe3d1a6cddbbc0e98672b219b4e1e68dfbc11423cc2624a",
  "basis": "Prepared seed prose and selected definition blocks; actual seeded SVG bytes, inspected through the three existing complete PNG previews and structured SVG coordinates. No answer source or prior choice artifact consulted. This record precedes creation of draft options.",
  "conventions": [
    "Blocks 7, 8 and 21 identify sequence positions as 1,2,3,... . No claim about zero membership in N is required.",
    "Blocks 15 and 18 require every adjacent inequality to be strict.",
    "For symbolic index tasks, record exactly when all referenced terms and predecessors exist; do not interpret undefined zeroth terms."
  ],
  "solutions": [
    {"source_number": "188", "solution": "Ordered teams: Westview, Dawn, Zenith, Eastside, Harvest. First Westview; last Harvest. Immediately after Westview is the second term Dawn; immediately before Eastside is the third term Zenith.", "tasks": ["Identify first and last values", "Identify both immediate neighbors and their positions"]},
    {"source_number": "189", "solution": "The sequence has six positions: Chemistry, Algebra, Literature, Literature, Drafting, PE. Third and fourth values are equal. Five distinct values do not reduce the six-term length.", "tasks": ["Count positions", "Detect repeated values without deduplicating the sequence"]},
    {"source_number": "190", "solution": "Each four-period schedule is a permutation of PE, English, Mathematics, Nature. Examples: PE-English-Mathematics-Nature; English-PE-Nature-Mathematics; Mathematics-Nature-English-PE. Every subject occurs once because there are four slots and all four must occur. There are 4!=24 possible schedules, although enumeration of all is not requested.", "tasks": ["Construct several ordered four-subject schedules", "Ensure all four subjects occur"]},
    {"source_number": "191", "solution": "a: X={1,2,3,4,5}, Y={1,3,5,7,9}, arrows 1->1,2->3,3->5,4->7,5->9 and ordered pairs (1,1),(2,3),(3,5),(4,7),(5,9). b: X={1,2,3,4,5,6}, Y={10,20,30}, arrows 1->10,2->20,3->30,4->10,5->20,6->30, corresponding pairs (1,10),(2,20),(3,30),(4,10),(5,20),(6,30). c: X={1,2,3,4,5,6}, Y={-1,1}, arrows 1->1,2->-1,3->1,4->-1,5->1,6->-1, pairs (1,1),(2,-1),(3,1),(4,-1),(5,1),(6,-1). Repeated values appear once in Y but repeated positions remain distinct in X.", "tasks": ["Determine each full domain and range", "Give each complete arrow correspondence", "Give each complete ordered-pair representation"]},
    {"source_number": "192", "solution": "a: two-digit multiples of 13 in increasing order are 13,26,39,52,65,78,91. Thus b_n=13n for 1<=n<=7 and b1=13,b4=52,b7=91. b: z_n=-1 for odd n and 0 for even n. Thus z1=-1,z4=0,z100=0,z253=-1,z2k=0,z2k+1=-1 wherever the indicated positive-index terms exist. If k=0 is included by a convention for N, z0 is undefined; this does not change any valid positive-index value.", "tasks": ["Find ordered two-digit multiples and requested terms", "Use parity for four numerical indices and both symbolic indices"]},
    {"source_number": "193", "solution": "Successors of a1,a4 are a2,a5. Predecessors of a2,a5 are a1,a4. a1 has no predecessor and a6 has no successor. Adjacency is about positions, not term magnitudes.", "tasks": ["Determine four immediate neighbors", "Identify both finite-sequence boundaries"]},
    {"source_number": "194", "solution": "a: successors are c11,c(k+1),c(k+4),c(k-1),c(2k+1). For all given starting terms to exist together k>=3 suffices. b: predecessors are c49,c(k-1),c(k+6),c(k-5),c(2k+2). Respectively c_k needs k>=2 and c_(k-4) needs k>=6; k>=6 makes every entry meaningful together. A successor adds 1 to the entire index, not to each occurrence of k.", "tasks": ["Apply successor rule to all five numerical/symbolic indices", "Apply predecessor rule to all five indices", "Respect positive-index existence conditions"]},
    {"source_number": "195", "solution": "a: b21,b22,b23,b24. b: b(k+1),b(k+2),b(k+3),b(k+4),b(k+5),b(k+6). c: b(k-1),b_k,b(k+1),b(k+2). Between excludes both endpoints. In c the first endpoint requires k>=3; in all cases the sequence must contain the named end terms.", "tasks": ["List every interior term in all three intervals", "Exclude endpoints and preserve order"]},
    {"source_number": "196", "solution": "In a sequence of length n, the kth from last has index n-k+1 for 1<=k<=n. Requested indices: n,n-1,n-4,n-k+1. The second requires n>=2; the fifth requires n>=5.", "tasks": ["Determine last, second-last, fifth-last and general kth-last indices", "State availability of reverse positions"]},
    {"source_number": "197", "solution": "Increasing: (b_n), since each appended 1 adds a positive decimal increment; (d_n), since n/(n+1)=1-1/(n+1) strictly increases. Decreasing: (a_n), since each step divides the positive term by 10; (c_n), since reciprocals of positive increasing integers decrease. Neither: (x_n) alternates up and down; (y_n) has equal adjacent terms, so is not strictly increasing, and also has rises, so is not decreasing.", "tasks": ["Classify all six sequences using strict adjacent comparisons"]},
    {"source_number": "198", "solution": "Examples: increasing 1,2,3,4; decreasing 4,3,2,1; neither 1,-1,1,-1. A constant sequence such as 2,2,2 is also neither under the strict definitions. Many valid constructions exist.", "tasks": ["Invent an example of each of the three categories"]},
    {"source_number": "199", "solution": "a: 7/12=0.583333... . Rounding gives x1=0.6,x2=0.58,x3=0.583,x10=0.5833333333. For n>=2, x_n is the truncation after n decimal places because the next digit is 3; each x_(n+1)>x_n, but x2<x1. No repeated values; the whole sequence is neither increasing nor decreasing. b: 23/45=0.511111... . Every rounding is truncation because the next digit is 1. y1=0.5 and each extra 1 increases the value. 0.5111111111 has ten fractional digits, so equals y10; (y_n) is strictly increasing.", "tasks": ["Convert both fractions to infinite decimals", "Round at all requested precisions", "Check equality of terms for x", "Classify both complete rounding sequences", "Infer y index from precision"]},
    {"source_number": "200", "solution": "a: the graph is exactly the six isolated points {(1,5),(2,3),(3,1),(4,-1),(5,-3),(6,-5)}. These lie on y=7-2x but the sequence graph is not the whole line or connected segments. b: exactly five isolated points {(1,-1),(2,3),(3,0),(4,-2),(5,4)}. Horizontal coordinates are positions beginning at 1, vertical coordinates are corresponding terms.", "tasks": ["Construct both complete discrete graphs", "Assign every ordered coordinate correctly", "Exclude interpolating edges and extra points"]},
    {"source_number": "201", "solution": "Actual SVG point heights, scale 16 px per value unit: a=-3,-1,0,2,2.5,5; b=-4.5,-8,-10.5,-12,-12.5,-12,-10.5,-8,-4.5; c=7,6,3,2,1,0.5,4; d=5,4.5,-2.5,-0.5,-1,-1.5. a strictly increases. b decreases then rises, c has c7>c6, d has d4>d3. Thus increasing set={a}; decreasing set is empty; b,c,d are neither. All points, rightmost panels and bottom negative points are visible in the single inspected 640x544 preview.", "tasks": ["Read all four graphs", "Determine complete increasing set", "Determine complete decreasing set without extrapolation"]},
    {"source_number": "202", "solution": "Let positive sides u,v satisfy u+v=20 and uv=S. Then t^2-20t+S=0. For S=84, discriminant 400-336=64, sides 6 cm and 14 cm, so yes. For S=105, discriminant 400-420=-20, so no. Equivalently S=100-(u-10)^2<=100 cm^2.", "tasks": ["Decide existence in both cases using simultaneous perimeter/area constraints"]},
    {"source_number": "203", "solution": "a: impose y=5-x. Substitution gives x^2+5=30, hence x=5 or -5; all points are (5,0),(-5,10). b: impose y=2x. Then x^2+8x-20=(x-2)(x+10)=0; all points are (2,4),(-10,-20). Both source singular phrasings have two real solutions, and neither may be discarded without an additional restriction.", "tasks": ["Solve each graph-plus-coordinate-condition system", "Retain both roots and matching y values in each case"]}
  ],
